/T (Weakly Typed : Think of weakly typed languages as individuals who are extremely agreeable. When you attempt to perform operations with completely different data types, they readily handle implicit type conversion on your behalf \(Doing all sort of things to keep the party going\))
/E (Weakly Typed : Think of weakly typed languages as individuals who are extremely agreeable. When you attempt to perform operations with completely different data types, they readily handle implicit type conversion on your behalf \(Doing all sort of things to keep the party going\))
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (Strongly Typed : On the other hand, strongly typed languages are the complete opposite. They do not compromise when it comes to type rules. If you want to add a string with an int, then you have do it explicitly. \(No implicit coercion of types\) and you cannot randomly cast any type to any other type, there are rules, strict rules.)
/E (Strongly Typed : On the other hand, strongly typed languages are the complete opposite. They do not compromise when it comes to type rules. If you want to add a string with an int, then you have do it explicitly. \(No implicit coercion of types\) and you cannot randomly cast any type to any other type, there are rules, strict rules.)
/Alt ()
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
